  test)
    # tomato test [--filter TEXT] [--timeout SECONDS] [--mode MODE] [-v] [FILE]... | FILE ENTRY [INPUTS...] (D81a, D97a). Each file in a tests/ folder is one test: a
    # program that passes when it ends with status 0 (nothing was raised) and fails otherwise; a file with functions marked
    # @test.NAME is run through the driver of the entry `test` (even if it has a @main), and passes when all of them do. Every file is built once and run as a
    # process of its own, so a sys.exit, a crash or a hang ends only that file. The words: one that ends in .tom or has a / is a file (a
    # folder stands for its *.tom files, except those whose name starts with _); a bare word before any file is an error (write x.tom).
    # After a file, a word that is not a file is the ENTRY to run in it, as `tomato run FILE ENTRY INPUTS...` (the entry may be a
    # dotted @test.NAME), and the words after it are the inputs of that entry's function; with no ENTRY the file runs as a whole
    # and takes no inputs.
    # With no word, every tests/*.tom. --filter TEXT keeps the files whose name contains TEXT; --timeout N stops a file that runs for
    # more than N seconds; -v also shows the output of the files that pass. One line for each file, then one line for CI; the status is
    # 1 when any file failed (did not compile, raised, ended with another status, timed out), else 0.
    tfilter=""; tmode=""; ttimeout=""; tverbose=0
